**Standard error of the sample mean (under H₀)**  

\[
SE_{\bar X}= \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
           = \frac{1.4}{\sqrt{40}}
           \approx \frac{1.4}{6.3249}
           \approx 0.2215 .
\]

So the “standard deviation” of the sampling distribution of the mean under the null hypothesis is roughly **0.22**.  
This value is what you would divide the observed mean‑difference by to obtain the t‑statistic:

\[
t=\frac{\bar x-\mu_0}{SE_{\bar X}}
   =\frac{98.2-98.6}{0.2215}\approx-1.81 .
\]
